      "writtenOn": "2025-09-23T15:30:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure what exactly you mean by \"boilerplate code\" and \"size mismatch\" in this case?\n\nAnd among the 5 arguments in https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/3666639/16/arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/mmu.c#95 only one argument (`bool writable`) is less than 8 bytes, i.e. those 5 arguments won\u0027t fit into 4 registers in any case, so we could only pass that struct by pointer, which would complicate the code a bit?\n\nBTW, as we recently discussed with Chuanxiao, in 6.15 possibly we will not need to pass the vcpu pointer as an argument to hypercalls (since pKVM itself can find out which vCPU is loaded on current CPU), including this MMU map hypercall, so possibly max 4 arguments will be enough and this patch will not be needed in 6.15. (But for 6.12 we are not planning that.)",
      "parentUuid": "11abf5fc_9f27102d",
      "revId": "85b46c509c3e35aef94cb3bd5335fe0758056a4c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c1166601_fa3826d0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-23T15:40:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This patch and the next patch is what I meant about the boiler plate code(code to support hypercalls that is needed in the mmu map cl). If we convert the arguments to a structure, we could avoid the extra parameter support:\n\n```\nstruct mmu_map_params {\n u64 gpa,\n u64 hpa,\n u64 size,\n bool writable\n };\n```\n\nAnd then we can pass the gpa of this struct to pkvm. I have seen precedence of this in linux kernel..\n\nRegarding the size mismatch, what I meant is passing smaller size variables in u64 may cause sideffects due to unintended or malicious invocation of hypercalls( for eg: pkvm expects a u32, but hypercall uses u64 and there is nothing that stops a caller from passing a u64 (either accidental or malicious)). With this existing implementation its not an issue, but I was generalizing it and saying this is one of the reason I prefer structures if we have arguments less than u64.",
      "parentUuid": "982572c8_505b25a2",
      "revId": "85b46c509c3e35aef94cb3bd5335fe0758056a4c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
